WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:02.085
CARTER MORGAN: Today,
it's time to figure out

00:00:02.085 --> 00:00:06.390
how Kubernetes actually
runs containers on nodes.

00:00:06.390 --> 00:00:07.200
Let's check it out.

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:07.650
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.650 --> 00:00:09.358
With a whole universe
of things to learn,

00:00:09.358 --> 00:00:11.640
it's important to start
with the essentials.

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:13.140
Let's go over the
concepts that make

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:16.410
Kubernetes usable, scalable,
and just downright awesome.

00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:17.880
Are you ready?

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:19.800
So with all of our
conversation about how

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:21.980
pods run in our
containers, I figured

00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:24.210
it would make sense
to talk about nodes,

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:27.510
which means it's time to
figure out, what are nodes?

00:00:27.510 --> 00:00:29.550
KASLIN FIELDS: That's
an important topic.

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:31.170
The simple way of
looking at it is

00:00:31.170 --> 00:00:33.870
that a node is a virtual
or physical machine

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:35.370
that you run your workloads on.

00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:36.162
CARTER MORGAN: Wow.

00:00:36.162 --> 00:00:37.650
That's pretty straightforward.

00:00:37.650 --> 00:00:39.420
And I know that
in Kubernetes, we

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:42.600
operate on clusters, which are
groups of one or more worker

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:43.217
nodes.

00:00:43.217 --> 00:00:45.300
But in order to get the
automation that Kubernetes

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:47.640
provides, I'm sure
nodes are more than just

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:49.580
any old virtual or
physical machine, right?

00:00:49.580 --> 00:00:51.060
KASLIN FIELDS: Exactly.

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:55.740
Each node actually contains the
services necessary to run pods.

00:00:55.740 --> 00:00:58.890
A container runtime
for running containers,

00:00:58.890 --> 00:01:01.170
a kubelet for making
sure that everything that

00:01:01.170 --> 00:01:05.010
should be running is,
and the kube-proxy

00:01:05.010 --> 00:01:06.120
for handling networking.

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:07.260
CARTER MORGAN: Ah-ha.

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:10.200
So that's how Kubernetes
keeps track of pods.

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:12.480
That also explains how
pods get IP endpoints

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:14.970
and other necessary features
for running at scale,

00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:17.928
like being able to attach to
a running pod for debugging.

00:01:17.928 --> 00:01:18.720
KASLIN FIELDS: Yep.

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:21.000
Now, if we had to
manually manage

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:25.260
all of the pods on each node, it
would be impossible to handle.

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:28.800
That's one of the most important
reasons Kubernetes exists.

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:30.870
The way Kubernetes
actually manages

00:01:30.870 --> 00:01:34.002
these pods is through what's
called the control plane.

00:01:34.002 --> 00:01:35.460
CARTER MORGAN: The
control plane is

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:37.860
responsible for handling
all of these details

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:39.690
by exposing an API.

00:01:39.690 --> 00:01:42.390
This is where Kubernetes can
define, deploy, and manage

00:01:42.390 --> 00:01:43.945
the lifecycle of our pods.

00:01:43.945 --> 00:01:44.820
KASLIN FIELDS: Right.

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:47.370
There's a lot going on
in the control plane.

00:01:47.370 --> 00:01:48.930
Let's look at some
of the components

00:01:48.930 --> 00:01:51.630
that make up the control
plane and what they do.

00:01:51.630 --> 00:01:54.730
First, there's the
API server itself,

00:01:54.730 --> 00:01:57.810
which handles data validation
and configuration for all

00:01:57.810 --> 00:01:59.700
of the API objects.

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:01.260
CARTER MORGAN:
Next, there's etcd,

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:03.180
which is a key-value
store for holding

00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:05.940
onto all the important
data that Kubernetes uses.

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:07.380
Plus, there's the Scheduler.

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:08.880
Here's where the
important decisions

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:11.340
get made about where
exactly a pod will run.

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:13.830
The Scheduler can look at the
available resources for all

00:02:13.830 --> 00:02:15.570
the nodes and make
sure that a pod goes

00:02:15.570 --> 00:02:17.120
to a node that can handle it.

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:19.287
KASLIN FIELDS: And don't
forget about the Controller

00:02:19.287 --> 00:02:22.470
Manager, where the core
Kubernetes logic happens.

00:02:22.470 --> 00:02:25.770
One of the big responsibilities
here is lifecycle management,

00:02:25.770 --> 00:02:29.430
to make sure all the various
pieces are working correctly.

00:02:29.430 --> 00:02:32.640
Similarly, there is the
Cloud Controller Manager,

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:35.470
which lets Kubernetes
hook into cloud providers.

00:02:35.470 --> 00:02:37.650
So if you're running
Google Kubernetes Engine,

00:02:37.650 --> 00:02:40.830
the Cloud Controller Manager
is what speaks to Google Cloud

00:02:40.830 --> 00:02:44.405
when something is needed, like a
new virtual machine for a node.

00:02:44.405 --> 00:02:46.530
CARTER MORGAN: Having that
abstraction from dealing

00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:49.410
with the individual details
of each cloud provider sure

00:02:49.410 --> 00:02:50.970
sounds nice.

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:51.840
Wow.

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:54.068
The control plane is
responsible for a lot.

00:02:54.068 --> 00:02:56.610
But with all of that, how do
you actually declare the details

00:02:56.610 --> 00:02:57.570
to Kubernetes?

00:02:57.570 --> 00:03:00.150
KASLIN FIELDS: That's where
the Kubernetes API comes in.

00:03:00.150 --> 00:03:02.192
Let's try it out in
the next episode.

00:03:02.192 --> 00:03:04.650
CARTER MORGAN: So that's how
Kubernetes does all the things

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:05.460
that it does.

00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:07.140
If you're going to
deploy a container,

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:09.330
you'd make a pod that
uses your container,

00:03:09.330 --> 00:03:11.040
you'd make sure
Kubernetes has nodes,

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:13.083
and then it figures
out the rest.

00:03:13.083 --> 00:03:14.250
KASLIN FIELDS: That's right.

00:03:14.250 --> 00:03:16.410
The kubelets on the
nodes are watching what's

00:03:16.410 --> 00:03:18.018
going on on the node itself.

00:03:18.018 --> 00:03:19.810
So when the control
plane needs to schedule

00:03:19.810 --> 00:03:22.680
a pod on that node, both the
control plane and the node

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:25.708
itself have enough information
to make sure it works.

00:03:25.708 --> 00:03:27.750
CARTER MORGAN: Plus, if
a pod runs into an issue,

00:03:27.750 --> 00:03:29.940
the control plane will
work with the kubelet

00:03:29.940 --> 00:03:34.080
to remove the unhealthy pod
and replace it with a new one.

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:34.680
Wow.

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:37.440
That was a lot of
great info on nodes.

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:41.100
But now, it's time to take a
deeper dive into the Kubernetes

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:42.060
API.

00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:42.690
All right.

00:03:42.690 --> 00:03:44.250
Stay tuned, everybody.

00:03:44.250 --> 00:03:46.260
If you want to get
hands-on, check out

00:03:46.260 --> 00:03:47.460
the link in the description.

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:50.010
And if you enjoyed the
episode, subscribe for more.

00:03:50.010 --> 00:03:53.360
